osened\")? The emitter still refuses when the inputs' `at` disagrees with the ledger's decided_at (`live_at != at` → refuse). The `decided_at` is gated by the ledger tool's bounds. This is arguably a new feature with new checks. The rule says \"Adding fields, new refusals, new checks or stricter checks is allowed.\" The question is whether any existing check got weaker.\n\nHmm, one potential concern: in the old emitter, `if live and at and (live.get(\"at\") != at or ...)` — compares ledger `at` to inputs `at`. In new emitter, compares `live_at` (decided_at or at) to inputs `at`. If a ledger line has `decided_at` different from what the inputs say... refuses. If the ledger line has `at` (tool clock) different from inputs' `at` — previously that refused (since the old dispose wrote at=t into both the ledger line and custody.json's disposition.at, so they always agreed unless hand-edited). Now, the inputs' `at` should equal `decided_at`. And the event `at` (tool clock) is no longer compared. Is that a loosening? The semantics changed: the ledger's authoritative time for the disposition is now `decided_at` (bounded), not `at` (the stamp clock). The emitter compares against the authoritative time. This isn't removing a check so much as tracking the new field. The ledger tool itself enforces `decided_at` bounds (refuses before last check, after clock). I think this is fine under \"new checks are allowed\".\n\nNow the big question is point 3, since `plumbline_verify.py` IS in the change.\n\n**Point 3: no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent, and the verifier cannot be bypassed at close (close must not succeed when the verifier fails).**\n\nLet's look at `plumbline_job.py do_close`. The relevant hunk:\n\n```python\nif doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:\n    print(\"\\ncustody:\")\n    cargs = [\"--job-dir\", str(job)]\n    if getattr(a, \"allow_hand_times\", False):\n        cargs.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", cargs)