-plumbline/v1 seems new with this change...) hmm, actually if the schema file is not in the change listing, maybe it's unchanged/pre-existing, or maybe it's just not shown. The diff header says \"The listing shows each changed file's hunks\" — only 4 files shown. The schema must pre-exist or be added elsewhere. Since SCHEMA_VERSION \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\" and the emitter writes `xp[\"schema\"]`, and the verifier enforces `xp.get(\"schema\") != SCHEMA_VERSION` → fail, plus validates against `$defs.xPlumbline`... \n\nI need to make a decision. Given the question's framing (\"Judge the change as a whole\"), and the strong design coherence (hand times refused by default, switch only gates that refusal, verifier recomputes everything and refuses absences via schema+checks, close refuses on verifier failure including cannot-run), I lean CONFORMS.\n\nBut wait — let me do one more pass for a planted violation, particularly around point 3(b): \"the verifier cannot be bypassed at close\".\n\nLook VERY carefully at do_close again:\n\n```python\n          if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:\n              print(\"\\ncustody:\")\n              cargs = [\"--job-dir\", str(job)]\n              if getattr(a, \"allow_hand_times\", False):\n                  cargs.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n              cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", cargs)\n              if cc != 0:\n                  why = (...)\n                  print(f\"\\nCLOSED, BUT THE CUSTODY RECORD IS {'INCOMPLETE' if cc == 1 else 'NOT WRITTEN'}: {why}; \"\n                        f\"re-emit with `plumbline_job.py custody`.\")\n                  return cc\n              vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)\n              if vc != 0:\n                  print(f\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed ({kind})\")\n                  log_row(job, \"close_refused\", {\"kind\": kind, \"exit\": vc}, a.run_log)\n                  return vc\n          log_row(job, \"closed\", {...})\n          print(f\"\\njob closed: {job