                "text": "Let me work through this carefully.\n\nThe RULE has three parts:\n\n1. No cross-check or refusal is loosened, removed or downgraded (wider tolerance, refusal turned into warning/gap, check skipped, early return, no longer reached).\n\n2. `--allow-hand-times`, if present, affects ONLY the refusal of hand-entered times (times no tool stamped). It must not skip, weaken or bypass any other check (order, cross-checks against logs, endpoint, approval, revision).\n\n3. If `kit/plumbline_verify.py` is in the change: no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent, and the verifier cannot be bypassed at close (close must not succeed when the verifier fails). If it is not in the change, this point does not apply.\n\nNow, the change does include `kit/plumbline_verify.py` (new file, shown whole). So point 3 applies.\n\nLet me examine each part.\n\n**Point 1: Are any cross-checks or refusals loosened, removed, or downgraded?**\n\nLooking at `custody_history.py`:\n\n- The old code had `fill_logger_times` which rewrote a pre-logger harness log in place, filling in missing `sent_at`/`received_at` from the response `created`/`Date` header. The new code makes `fill_logger_times` a no-op (deprecated, \"the emitter never writes its inputs (T7)\"). This changes behavior: previously calls with no logger times got the times written into the log, so `read_log` would see `sent_at`/`received_at` present. Now such calls are read as \"hand times\": their time comes from the Date header or created epoch.\n\n- New behavior: \"The emitter never writes it back to the log; it refuses it by default, and under `--allow-hand-times` lists it in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]`.\"\n\nSo previously: a call without logger stamps got the log rewritten (fill_logger_times), and then the times came from the log itself (stamped by the rewrite). Now: the emitter refuses by default (a new refusal — stricter), and `--allow-hand-times` allows writing the record with the hand times flagged.\n\nWait — is this a loosening? Under the old code, the emitter would... hmm. Let's think. Under the old code, the emitter called `H.fill_logger_times(log)` before reading, so the log had `sent_at`/`received_at` written from the call's own response created/Date header. Then the emitter used those times. There was no refusal — the times were silently sourced from the server Date header/created epoch (which, per the docstring, are \"exactly the times the emitter already used\").\n\nUnder the new code, by default the emitter refuses (exit 3) when there's a hand time. Under `--allow-hand-times`, it writes the record listing the hand times. So compared to the old behavior, this is stricter by default (new refusal), and the `--allow-hand-times` switch permits what was previously the default behavior — but now flagged and recorded.\n\nIs `--allow-hand-times` itself a \"loosening\" under point 1? Point 1 says no cross-check or refusal is loosened, removed or downgraded — \"for example: a wider tolerance, a refusal turned into a warning or a gap, a check that is skipped, returned from early, or no longer reached\". Point 2 explicitly allows `--allow-hand-times` to exist provided it affects ONLY the refusal of hand-entered times (times no tool stamped). So the hand-time refusal being switchable is sanctioned by point 2, as long as it only affects hand-entered times (times no tool stamped) and doesn't skip other checks.\n\nSo the key question for point 2: does `--allow-hand-times` only affect the hand-time refusal?\n\nLet me trace through `emit_custody.py build()`:\n\n- `hand: dict[str, dict] = {}` — collects hand times via `mark()`.\n- `mark` is called for: `bar.approvals[i].at` (when the live ledger event's writer is hand/no-writer, or when no ledger event and the inputs carry the time), `bar.frozenAt` similarly, `startedAt`/`producedAt` typed into custody.json, `routing.<seat>.declaredAt` (hand-written pin), `passes.<pid>.at` typed, `controls[id].at` typed, `disposition.at` similarly, and via `flag_calls`, every harness call whose `sent_at`/`received_at` the logger did not stamp.\n- Then at the end:\n```python\nxp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]\nif hand and allow_hand:\n    xp[\"allowances\"] = {...}\nif hand and not allow_hand:\n    for e in hand.values():\n        ref.add(\"hand-time\", f\"{e['field']} {e['file']}:{e['line']} ({e['source']})\")\n```\n\nSo `allow_hand` only controls whether the hand-time refusal is added. All other refusals (`ref.add` with kinds \"time\", \"order\", \"endpoint\", \"approval\") are added regardless of `allow_hand`. Let me verify: search for any place where `allow_hand` or `hand` gates a non-hand-time check.\n\n- `if hand and not allow_hand: ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)` — only place `allow_hand` is used in `build`.\n- `main`: `build(..., allow_hand=a.allow_hand_times)` — passed through.\n- `flag_calls(calls, hand)`: `if hand is None: return` — hand is never None in build (it's a dict). This just adds entries to the hand dict; it doesn't skip any checks. The calls are still read, still filtered by `c.at`, still cross-checked via `H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)`, still annotated, pin order checked.\n\nWait — one subtlety: in `maker_history`, before:\n\n```python\nH.fill_logger_times(log)\ncalls = H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")\n```\n\nafter:\n\n```python\nlog_calls = H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")\nflag_calls(log_calls, hand)\ncalls = log_calls\n```\n\nThe `fill_logger_times` call is removed. Does that remove a check? `fill_logger_times` wrote times into the log; it wasn't a check. Removing it means the log is no longer rewritten — that's stricter in the sense that un-stamped calls now surface as hand times and are refused by default. But does removing the fill change any cross-check? Under the old flow, the cross-check compared the hand-entered time against the call's time which came from the filled log (same created/Date values). Under the new flow, the cross-check compares against `call.at` which reads Date header/created directly (the `Call.at` property falls back to Date header then created — see lines 180-186). So the cross-check still happens with effectively the same values. Not loosened.\n\nHmm, but wait: there's a subtle issue. In the old flow, `fill_logger_times` wrote `sent_at`/`received_at` into the log, so `read_log` would find them and `Call.sent`/`Call.received` would be set from those values... Actually wait, no. `fill_logger_times` computed `send, recv = _clock(resp.get(\"created\"), Date)`, i.e. whole-second ISO strings, and wrote them as `sent_at`/`received_at`. Then `read_log` would parse them. `Call.started` returns `source_of(\"sent_at\")` if `self.sent` else... Let me look at the context lines 180-186:\n\n```python\nif self.received:\n    return self.source_of(\"received_at\")\nn = self._line_of(_hdr(...Date...), \"Date\")\nif n:\n    return f\"{self.log}:{n}\"\nn = self._line_of((self.raw.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"created\"), \"created\")\nreturn f\"{self.log}:{n}\" if n else None\n```\n\nThat's `source_at` presumably. So `Call.at` falls back to Date/created when `received` is None. Fine.\n\nNow, is there any check that previously ran and now doesn't? Let's compare `read_log`: it's now `read_log(path, ref=None, who=\"log\")` with optional ref — \"Every caller that passes its own Refusals keeps working unchanged\". The internal checks (ref.add for neither Date nor created; created vs Date self-contradiction check with TOLERANCE_S) are unchanged (context lines 249-253).\n\nThe `-` block removed `_clock` and the body of `fill_logger_times` — replaced with a no-op. `fill_logger_times` was a write, not a check. OK.\n\nNow `custody_ledger.py`: lots of new refusals and subcommands. The `dispose` command changed: previously `dispose --by Name` wrote `ci[\"disposition\"][\"at\"] = t` (tool clock) — and the emitter compared the ledger's `at` to the inputs' `at`. Now `dispose --decided-at T` writes `decided_at` into `disposition.at` in custody.json and keeps the tool clock in the event's `at`, with bounds checks: `decided_at` must not be before the last logged check and not after the tool's clock; requires `--decided-source`. These are new refusals (stricter). The emitter's cross-check now compares `live_at = live.get(\"decided_at\") or live.get(\"at\")` against the inputs' `at`. Hmm — is this a loosening? Previously the comparison was `live.get(\"at\") != at`. Now the ledger line's `at` is the tool clock and `decided_at` is the recorded decision time; the emitter compares the record's disposition.at against decided_at. A real disagreement still refuses (kind `time`). Since the ledger tool itself changed what it writes (at = tool clock, decided_at = bounded decision time), comparing against decided_at is the correct cross-check, and the tool bounds decided_at between the last check and its own clock. This seems fine — new checks, not removed ones.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety: with `dispose` without `--decided-at`, `decided_at = t` and `disposition.at = t`, `ev.at = t` — same as before. With `--decided-at`, the event's `at` is the tool clock `t`, and `decided_at` is bounded. The emitter uses `live_at = decided_at or at`. Could someone abuse `--decided-at` to type a hand time? They'd have to pass `--decided-at T --decided-source S`, and the ledger tool bounds T: not before last check, not after tool clock. But T is still hand-entered... but the tool stamps it as `decided_at` with `decided_source` and its own `writer` stamp and `at`. Is `disposition.at` then a \"hand time\"? In the emitter, `live` exists with `writer` = the tool stamp, so `writer_source(live)` returns None → not marked as hand time. Hmm. But this is a design decision: the ledger tool recorded the decision time with a source, bounded by checks. The record's `disposition.at` gets `decided_at` which is cross-checked/bounded. Is this a violation of point 1 (\"no cross-check or refusal is loosened\")? The emitter still refuses when the inputs' `at` disagrees with the ledger's decided_at (`live_at != at` → refuse). The `decided_at` is gated by the ledger tool's bounds. This is arguably a new feature with new checks. The rule says \"Adding fields, new refusals, new checks or stricter checks is allowed.\" The question is whether any existing check got weaker.\n\nHmm, one potential concern: in the old emitter, `if live and at and (live.get(\"at\") != at or ...)` — compares ledger `at` to inputs `at`. In new emitter, compares `live_at` (decided_at or at) to inputs `at`. If a ledger line has `decided_at` different from what the inputs say... refuses. If the ledger line has `at` (tool clock) different from inputs' `at` — previously that refused (since the old dispose wrote at=t into both the ledger line and custody.json's disposition.at, so they always agreed unless hand-edited). Now, the inputs' `at` should equal `decided_at`. And the event `at` (tool clock) is no longer compared. Is that a loosening? The semantics changed: the ledger's authoritative time for the disposition is now `decided_at` (bounded), not `at` (the stamp clock). The emitter compares against the authoritative time. This isn't removing a check so much as tracking the new field. The ledger tool itself enforces `decided_at` bounds (refuses before last check, after clock). I think this is fine under \"new checks are allowed\".\n\nNow the big question is point 3, since `plumbline_verify.py` IS in the change.\n\n**Point 3: no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent, and the verifier cannot be bypassed at close (close must not succeed when the verifier fails).**\n\nLet's look at `plumbline_job.py do_close`. The relevant hunk:\n\n```python\nif doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:\n    print(\"\\ncustody:\")\n    cargs = [\"--job-dir\", str(job)]\n    if getattr(a, \"allow_hand_times\", False):\n        cargs.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n    cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", cargs)\n    if cc != 0:\n        ...print CLOSED, BUT THE CUSTODY RECORD IS ...; return cc\n    vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)\n    if vc != 0:\n        print(f\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed ({kind})\")\n        log_row(job, \"close_refused\", {\"kind\": kind, \"exit\": vc}, a.run_log)\n        return vc\n    log_row(job, \"closed\", ...)\n    print(f\"\\njob closed: {job}\")\n    if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") not in TERMINALS:\n        print(...)\nreturn code\n```\n\nWait, let me re-read the hunk carefully:\n\n```\n       -         log_row(job, \"closed\", {\"mode\": doc.get(\"mode\"), \"outcome\": doc.get(\"outcome\"),\n       -                                 \"disposition_state\": state}, a.run_log)\n       -         print(f\"\\njob closed: {job}\")\n  243           if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:\n  244               print(\"\\ncustody:\")\n       -             cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", [\"--job-dir\", str(job)])\n  245 +             cargs = [\"--job-dir\", str(job)]\n  246 +             if getattr(a, \"allow_hand_times\", False):\n  247 +                 cargs.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")\n  248 +             cc = call(\"emit_custody.py\", cargs)\n  249               if cc != 0:\n  250                   why = (...)\n  253                   print(f\"\\nCLOSED, BUT THE CUSTODY RECORD IS ...\")\n  254                   return cc\n       -         else:\n  255 +             vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)\n  256 +             if vc != 0:\n  257 +                 print(f\"NOT CLOSED: Plumbline verify failed ({kind})\")\n  258 +                 log_row(job, \"close_refused\", {\"kind\": kind, \"exit\": vc}, a.run_log)\n  259 +                 return vc\n  260 +         log_row(job, \"closed\", {\"mode\": doc.get(\"mode\"), \"outcome\": doc.get(\"outcome\"),\n  261 +                                 \"disposition_state\": state}, a.run_log)\n  262 +         print(f\"\\njob closed: {job}\")\n  263 +         if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") not in TE