ter than before (before, if it agreed within tolerance, no refusal). Wait — was there a refusal before? Before, cross_check refuses when |difference| > TOLERANCE_S. If within tolerance, the typed time was fine (record uses log's time anyway). Now, the typed time is ALSO a hand time (refused unless --allow-hand-times). So this change makes things STRICTER: previously-acceptable typed times (within tolerance) now require --allow-hand-times. Stricter is allowed (\"new refusals... or stricter checks is allowed\"). And --allow-hand-times then permits them, flagged. Is that within point 2's allowance? \"--allow-hand-times... affects ONLY the refusal of hand-entered times (times no tool stamped)\". A typed `passes.at` even when a call is linked — is that \"a time no tool stamped\"? The typed value in custody.json has no tool behind it (the tool's time is the log's). The record carries the log's time; the typed value was only a cross-check input. Flagging it as a hand time and allowing it under the switch... The switch's effect: without it, refuse; with it, list in hand_times[]. It doesn't change any other check — the cross_check still runs, the record still uses the log's time. OK.\n\n- Controls: same pattern.\n- Disposition: live_at = decided_at or at; cross-check refuses on disagreement (kind time) — retained; marks hand times.\n- `check_order(pred, packet, ci, now, ref)` — unchanged (context).\n- End: `xp[\"hand_times\"]`, allowances when allow_hand, refusals when not allow_hand. `xp[\"generations\"] = ledger_array(lines, \"generation\")` etc. — new fields. `xp[\"spend\"]`, `xp[\"inputs\"]` (carrying the inputs' own x-plumbline verbatim), gaps.\n\n- `main`: `--allow-hand-times` flag → `build(..., allow_hand=a.allow_hand_times)`. Refusal printing unchanged (kind filter? no — prints all ref.rows). Exit 3 on any refusals. So with allow_hand, only the hand-time refusals are absent; every other refusal still fires.\n\nWait — one thing to double check: `flag_calls(calls, hand