osed` row, a `close_refused` row carrying the failure kind\". So per the change's own documentation, the close is refused. Whether the packet write happens before... we can't see. The visible code logs `close_refused` and returns vc. I'll take the code at face value: close refused.\n\n**B. `run_verifier` `kind` parsing:** cosmetic.\n\n**C. The verifier's `standard_check`**: runs the pinned checker FIRST; if it fails → exit 3, nothing else checked. That's layering, fine. If checker missing/pin mismatch → exit 2 (cannot run) → close refused. Good — can't bypass by removing the checker.\n\n**D. `snapshot` before/after**: detects writes to inputs during verification → \"writes\" failure. Extra check.\n\n**E. `find_pin` / `_find_pin_in`**: this looks a bit loose — `if vv.startswith(\"aaeb7e05\") and vv.endswith(\"bcd18\")` — a hardcoded partial sha match?? That looks like a planted backdoor-ish thing... but it's about which checker sha the pin names, i.e., ensuring the checker bytes match the pin. `_find_pin_in(doc, sha)` searches pin.json for the sha of the checker file found on disk. The weird partial-match `startswith(\"aaeb7e05\") and endswith(\"bcd18\")` — hmm, this would return `vv` (the full string from the doc) if the doc's value starts with those 8 hex chars and ends with those 5. Then `standard_check` compares `file_sha(check) != pin` → the actual file sha must equal that value. So the partial match only matters for WHICH pin value is selected when multiple candidates exist; the bytes still must hash to it. A 13-hex-char partial match as a \"pin\" — the file would need sha256 exactly equal to that string, which is 13 chars ≠ 64 hex... wait, `_find_pin_in` returns `vv` which could be a 13-char string; then `file_sha(check) != pin` compares a 64-char hex to a 13-char string → never equal → exit 2. So that branch can only cause \"cannot run\", not bypass. Unless the doc value IS the full sha that happens to start/end with those. Weird but not a bypass. It's suspicious-looking but fails closed. Not a rule violation per se (rule is about custody checks, not checker pinning aesthetics).\n\n**F. THE BIG ONE — let me re-examine `do_close`'s emit-failure path vs the rule**: \"close must not succeed when the verifier fails\" — verifier only runs if emit succeeded. Fine.\n\n**G. Let me re-examine `emit_custody.py` for any check that got downgraded.** \n\nThe disposition cross-check: original `if live and at and (live.get(\"at\") != at or (live.get(\"by\") and dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\")))`. New: `live_at = (live.get(\"decided_at\") or live.get(\"at\")) if live else None; if live and at and (live_at != at or ...)`. Consider a ledger dispose line WITHOUT decided_at (base form): live_at = live[\"at\"] — same as original. With decided_at: compares decided_at. The ledger's `at` (tool clock) is no longer compared to the inputs' `at`. Could that hide a misstatement? The inputs' `at` should be decided_at (what `dispose --decided-at` wrote into custody.json). If someone hand-edits custody.json's disposition.at to a different time T', the emitter refuses (T' != decided_at). If someone hand-edits BOTH custody.json and the ledger line... the ledger file digest... there's no digest chain on the ledger (events_log takes latest row per key). Pre-existing weakness. Not a change-introduced loosening.\n\nHmm — wait, actually there IS something. Original: `if live and not at and live.get(\"at\"): at = live.get(\"at\")`. New: `if live and not at and live_at: at = live_at`. Same shape.\n\n**H. `check_approval` call in custody_ledger approve** — unchanged (context).\n\n**I. In `emit_custody.py` main**: exit-code docstring — \"3 refused because the record would misstate its own history: ... a hand time with no --allow-hand-times ...\". And \"Nothing is written on 2 or 3\". Confirmed by code: `if ref: print... return 3` presumably before writing. Context shows the print block; the return 3 must follow (cut off at 1211). Fine.\n\n**J. Now, `flag_calls` — \"Every call counts, not only the first and the last, and both fields\".** In maker_history: `calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]` — filtering happens AFTER flag_calls(log_calls, hand) — wait:\n\n```python\nlog_calls = H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")\nflag_calls(log_calls, hand)\ncalls = log_calls\nif m.get(\"calls\"):\n    calls = [c for c in calls if c.name in m[\"calls\"]]\ncalls = [c for c in calls if c.at]\n```\n\nSo flagging happens on ALL log calls before filtering — every call the logger didn't stamp is flagged, even calls excluded by m[\"calls\"] or without `at`. Good (stricter, complete flagging).\n\nIn evaluator_history: `flag_calls(log_calls, hand); calls = [c for c in log_calls if c.at]`. Same.\n\n**K. `hand_time_set` in the verifier vs `hand` in the emitter — do they agree?** The verifier recomputes hand times: ledger lines with hand/no-writer writers (freeze/approve/dispose/pin), inputs-typed values when no ledger line, typed startedAt/producedAt/passes/controls, and un-stamped call fields from ALL *.log files in evidence dirs. The emitter marks the same categories. The verifier's `hand_time_set` marks `bar.approvals[i].at` only for approvals matching roles... and pops when the ledger line has a tool writer. Looks symmetric with the emitter. If they diverge, hand-time-unflagged/flag-without-source failures — stricter, fine.\n\n**L. Now the schema check `check_schema` early exit:** `if failures: return finish(4, failures, a.json)` — schema failures exit 4 before running other checks. \"No verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent\" — schema failure IS a failure; exiting early with it is fine (the close still fails).\n\n**M. Hmm, wait — let me recheck `check_shadow`'s `inputs` shadow check:**\n\n```python\ninputs = xp.get(\"inputs\")\nif not isinstance(inputs, dict): inputs = {}\n...\nfor key in OWNED:\n    if key in inputs and key in xp and key in derived:\n        if xp.get(key) == inputs.get(key) and xp.get(key) != derived[key]:\n            fails.append(shadow x-plumbline.inputs.{key})\n```\n\nThis catches the inputs carrying a stale copy. Extra check.\n\n**N. Let me look again at `hand_time_set`'s approval handling:**\n\n```python\nelif ev == \"approve\":\n    role = e.get(\"role\")\n    seen.add((\"approve\", role))\n    for i, a in enumerate(...approvals):\n        if a.get(\"role\") == role:\n            f = f\"bar.approvals[{i}].at\"\n            if src: mark(f, ledger_rel, n, src)\n            else: hand.pop(f, None)\n    continue\n```\n\nMatches emitter. OK.\n\n**O. Verifier exit code 2 paths**: record not found, ledger not found, custody dir not found, bar not found, checker issues, record not JSON. All exit 2 → close refused. Note: `--json` optional. Fine.\n\n**P. Wait — one more on do_close: `run_verifier(job, a)` uses `a` from the CLOSE parser which now has `--custody`. But what about `$PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY`? Handled. If neither → (2, \"cannot-run\") → refused. So the close CANNOT succeed without running the verifier. \n\n**Q. Hmm, now let me double-check `do_custody` verifier record path when `--out-dir` given:** rec = out_dir/job_id.custody.json. If emit wrote elsewhere... it writes DIR/<job_id>.custody.json per docstring. Fine.\n\n**R. Now — is there anything in the change that turns a refusal into a gap or warning?** Search emit_custody for `gaps.add` where ref.add used to be... The diff doesn't show such conversions. The `relay_only_buyer` adds a refusal. The hand-time mechanism adds refusals gated by the switch (sanctioned).\n\n**S. TOLERANCE_S unchanged** (60 s base, referenced in shadow's derived time_tolerance_s = H.TOLERANCE_S). No widening visible.\n\n**T. One more careful look at the removal in emit_custody around lines 812-834 (disposition):** the original refused `live.get(\"at\") != at`. New refuses `live_at != at` where live_at prefers decided_at. Suppose a ledger line has decided_at=X and at=Y (tool clock), and inputs say at=X: original code would refuse (Y != X); new code passes. Is that a \"loosening\"? The ledger tool CHANGED its write format in the same change: it now writes at=Y (clock) and decided_at=X into custody.json's disposition.at. So inputs.at = X by construction. Under the old tool + old emitter, inputs.at would have been Y. The comparison target changed consistently with the tool's format. The bounded decided_at is checked by the tool (>= last check, <= clock). So the record's disposition.at = X is bounded-checked, stamped by the tool with a writer stamp and a source. Not a hand time (tool stamped). I judge: not a violation; it's the new check regime, with new refusals added.\n\n**U. `last_check_time`** — used for the decided_at lower bound; best-effort but a new check.\n\n**V. What about `ci_changed = a.cmd in (\"freeze\",\"approve\",\"pin\",\"dispose\")` and `write_custody` — previously the ledger ALWAYS wrote custody.json (even for... well, only those commands existed). Same set. Fine.\n\n**W. `show` command unchanged.**\n\n**X. RefusingParser.error** — exit 2 with REFUSED: line. Previously argparse exited 2 without the REFUSED prefix. Stricter formatting. Fine.\n\n**Y. `emit_custody.py`: `mk = maker_history(...)` now passes `hand`; `startedAt`/`producedAt` typed → marked. Note: `xp[\"times\"] = {\"startedAt\": \"harness log\" if mk.get(\"startedAt\") else \"hand-entered\", ...}` unchanged.\n\n**Z. `check_order(pred, packet, ci, now, ref)` unchanged; `H.cross_check` calls unchanged.**\n\nHmm, okay. Now let me reconsider the one thing that nags me: **`check_hand_times` early return when `hand_times` is not a list.** Combined with `check_shadow` skipping keys absent from xp, if the xPlumbline schema does NOT require `hand_times`, a malicious record could omit hand_times entirely and pass verification despite unflagged hand times. But the schema file is part of the repo (`schemas/x-plumbline/x-plumbline-v1.schema.json`) and NOT shown in the diff — meaning it's either unchanged (pre-existing, but x-