t 16, `else:` at 12 (removed), `print(\"(no custody record...\")` at 16.\n\nNew: after `if cc != 0: ... return cc` (12/16), `vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)` at 12 (line 255 shows 12 spaces? `  255 +             vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)` — after `255 + ` there are 12 spaces → yes 12). `if vc != 0:` 12, body 16. Then line 260 `log_row(job, \"closed\", ...` — `  260 +         log_row(` — after `260 + ` there are 8 spaces → 8-space indent. So `log_row(closed)` and `print(job closed)` are at 8, OUTSIDE the `if terminal` block, i.e., they run for BOTH terminal and non-terminal closes — but only AFTER the terminal branch's emit+verify passed. Structure:\n\n```python\n        if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:   # 8\n            print custody; emit; if cc: return cc    # 12\n            vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)          # 12\n            if vc != 0:                              # 12\n                close_refused; return vc             # 16\n        log_row(job, \"closed\", ...)                  # 8\n        print(f\"\\njob closed: {job}\")                # 8\n        if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") not in TERMINALS:  # 8\n            print(\"(no custody record...)\")          # 12\n    return code                                      # 4\n```\n\nGood — so `closed` row is logged after verification passes (for terminal) or directly (for non-terminal). And on emitter failure (cc != 0): return cc WITHOUT closed row — changed from original (which logged closed first). Hmm — so now, if custody emit fails, no `closed` row is written even though the packet is terminal. The message still says \"CLOSED, BUT THE CUSTODY RECORD IS NOT WRITTEN\". Is this a violation of anything? Point 3 says close must not succeed when the verifier fails — here the verifier never ran because emit failed; close does not succeed (non-zero, no closed row). That's MORE strict, not less. Though it contradicts the pre-existing docstring (\"the close stands\")... the docstring at lines