ays the Architect writes the standard and the Owner ratifies the frame. Is that a contradiction? The guide is about a practice where the Orchestrator seems to take the Architect role too — line 21-23 discloses combined seats: \"If one seat writes an instrument and also runs the gate it is judged by... say so in the record's separationGaps.\" And the story says \"the Orchestrator wrote the bar and the instruments\". The doctrine allows one person to hold several seats if disclosed (§3: \"One person or model may hold several seats if that is disclosed, with one exception that is never waived: the Maker never judges its own work.\"). So the Orchestrator acting as Architect is permissible when disclosed, and the guide explicitly requires disclosure (line 21). Not a contradiction.\n\nLine 15: \"You are not the Maker and not the Evaluator. You never write the fix, never make the Maker's model calls, never score.\" Consistent with doctrine §3 (Orchestrator never writes the work or scores it). Good.\n\nLine 99: \"**The best artifact is the input.** Once the job has produced an artifact, the next iteration's Maker is given the best artifact the job has produced... The sources are e-di Instrument rule 12 (I24) and the doctrine's rule to carry the best work forward.\"\n\nDoctrine Principle 9: \"Carry the best work forward: a revision replaces the incumbent only if it is better on at least one dimension and worse on none.\" And the exits: \"The best version the job produced goes to the Owner\". The guide's \"best artifact is the input\" is consistent — giving the Maker the best artifact so far plus known defects. Does this contradict doctrine Principle 9 (\"Inside a loop, a change answers only what the Evaluator reported\")? Hmm. The guide gives the Maker \"every known workable solution with the measurement behind it\" — the doctrine says the Maker revises against classified feedback, and \"Nothing else goes back\" (§5 step 5: \"send the classified shortfall back to the Maker a