                "text": "Let me analyze this carefully. The question asks whether the document conforms to the rule, which includes:\n\nA. The Owner's decisions of 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT:\n- A1. Repeat-failure tripwire: when the same Maker failure class occurs 3 times (exactly three, not \"a few\" or another number), the Maker stops retrying and the request is changed (or reported back). Maker attempts are still not iterations.\n- A2. Spend: the job keeps the shared API key, protected by the harness's pre-call spend guard; because the key is shared, key-usage reconciliation is only an upper bound on the job's spend. The document must not recommend or require a per-job key as the rule.\n- A3. The cross-family pre-read is optional, decided per job on the approval sheet (not mandatory, not dropped).\n\nB. Standing rules:\n- B1. finish_reason `length` with empty content is its own failure class: stop at the first one and change the request; do not retry it as is.\n- B2. No throttling: max output tokens = the endpoint's maximum and no reasoning limit; tasks are sized so they can finish; finish_reason is logged on every call.\n- B3. The cap counts E-DI iterations, not Maker attempts or generations.\n- B4. Handoffs name the command that writes files (not only a check-only command).\n- B5. Handoffs are checked against the standing rules before they are sent.\n- B6. A live test of each model-calling instrument, at the frozen settings, is a freeze gate.\n- B7. Evidence is write-once (never edited after it is written; corrections go beside it).\n- B8. A relayed approval is a report, not an approval; only the Owner's own words approve.\n- B9. No job work in /tmp.\n\nC. The doctrine. The guide may add practice, but nothing it says may contradict the doctrine, and it must say that the doctrine wins on conflict.\n\nThe check: A statement contradicts a rule if a reader following the document would act against the rule. Leaving out a rule is not a contradiction for this check (presence is checked elsewhere); stating its opposite is.\n\nNow let me go through the document line by line for contradictions.\n\nLine 3: \"Nothing here overrides any of them, and where this guide and the doctrine disagree, the doctrine wins.\" — Good, satisfies C's requirement that the doctrine wins on conflict.\n\nLine 17: \"Relayed approvals are not approvals. Only the Owner's own words approve anything.\" — Matches B8.\n\nLine 27: \"**The cap counts E-DI iterations.**\" — Matches B3.\n\nLine 29: \"Maker-internal attempts (retries, follow-up calls, regenerations inside one Maker turn) are not iterations.\" — Matches A1's \"Maker attempts are still not iterations.\"\n\nLine 30: \"**The tripwire (Owner decision, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT):** when the **same failure class** comes up **3 times**, the Maker stops retrying and must **change the request**.\" — Matches A1: exactly 3 times, stops retrying, changes request. Good.\n\nLine 31: \"The tripwire does not cap spend; the budget does.\" — Fine.\n\nLine 33: 'Write the cap in the bar in one sentence: \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the tripwire.\"' — Fine.\n\nLine 42: \"No throttling. No reasoning limit, and max output tokens at the endpoint max\" — Matches B2.\n\nLine 45: \"The endpoint max is necessary, not sufficient: size each task so it finishes inside the maximum... log finish_reason on every call\" — Matches B2.\n\nLine 48: \"**finish_reason = length with empty content is its own failure class. Stop at the first one.** Do not wait for the tripwire's third time... Change the request\" — Matches B1. \"A changed request is a new Maker attempt, not an iteration\" — fine.\n\nLine 50: \"Run a live test of every model-calling instrument at the exact frozen settings before freezing, and make the live run a freeze gate.\" — Matches B6.\n\nLine 54: \"The runbook must name the command that writes the files, not only the one that checks them.\" — Matches B4.\n\nLine 56: \"Check every handoff against the standing rules before you send it.\" — Matches B5.\n\nLine 62: \"Enforce spend in the harness, not in prose... The job keeps the shared key behind a pre-call spend guard\" — Matches A2.\n\nLine 63: \"**Owner decision, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT:** the job keeps the shared key, guarded before every paid call, rather than taking a per-job key. Reconciliation against key usage is therefore an upper bound, not an exact figure, and the record must say so. This guide does not recommend a per-job key; the Owner weighed that option and chose the shared key.\" — Matches A2 explicitly. Good.\n\nLine 67: \"Decide the cross-family pre-read per job, on the approval sheet. A pre-read by a model family not already in the job is optional...\" — Matches A3.\n\nLine 68: \"*Owner decision, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT:* the pre-read is optional and is chosen per job.\" — Matches A3.\n\nLine 89: \"Logged files are write-once by content.\" — Matches B7.\n\nLine 144: \"Keep logged files write-once, by content. Before an event cites a file, copy it to evidence/<name>-<hash8>.<ext> and cite the copy. A correction is a new file and a new event, never an edit.\" — Matches B7.\n\nLine 116: \"**Never put job work in /tmp.** /tmp is wiped on reboot.\" — Matches B9.\n\nNow let me check more carefully for subtle contradictions.\n\nLine 30: The tripwire — \"when the same failure class comes up 3 times, the Maker stops retrying and must change the request. A different failure class starts its own count. Log every attempt with its class: finish_reason = length with empty content, a diff or extraction that will not apply, the same test failing, a provider refusal.\"\n\nWait — B1 says finish_reason `length` with empty content is its own failure class: stop at the FIRST one and change the request; do not retry it as is. Line 48 says exactly that: \"Stop at the first one. Do not wait for the tripwire's third time.\" Good — consistent.\n\nBut line 30 lists \"finish_reason = length with empty content\" as one of the classes logged with the tripwire. Does that contradict B1? Line 30 says log every attempt with its class, including length-with-empty-content. Line 48 clarifies that for this class you stop at the first one rather than waiting for the tripwire's third. So the document is internally consistent and consistent with B1. A reader following the document would not retry length-with-empty-content as is. Line 48 explicitly says \"Do not wait for the tripwire's third time\" and \"Change the request\". OK.\n\nLine 101: \"**Keep finished parts.** After an interruption or a failed iteration, the Maker never throws away completed, acceptable work (Owner rule of 2026-10-28 19:00 EDT, reaffirmed by the Owner on 2026-10-29 07:42 EDT)...\"\n\nHmm, wait. This references Owner rules dated 2026-10-28 19:00 EDT and 2026-10-29 07:42 EDT. The document version is \"Version of 2026-10-28\" (line 5). Interesting but not necessarily a contradiction. Actually, hold on — line 5 says \"Version of 2026-10-28\" but the document references decisions from 2026-10-29. That's an internal inconsistency but not a contradiction of the rule being checked. Not relevant to the check.\n\nLine 102: \"It was ruled LOST, which is not a failure class and does not count toward the tripwire. The ledger and emitter commits already made were kept, and the Maker resumed at the verifier part only — attempt 2, with byte-identical messages, after the Owner's own yes in the Maker's chat.\"\n\nHmm — \"resumed... with byte-identical messages\" — is that a retry of the same request? The tripwire says when the same failure class comes up 3 times, stop and change the request. A LOST call is not a failure class, so retrying identical messages doesn't violate the tripwire. And B1 concerns finish_reason length with empty content — a lost call has no reply. So no contradiction.\n\nLine 46: \"It failed five times with finish_reason = length at 131,072 tokens... Batching fixed it\". This is a story about the ev