vidence kept with work. \"Record the base run as a deviation outside the repo\" — hmm, that says record as a deviation, outside the repo. Does that contradict the doctrine's evidence rules? The doctrine says every claim cites a file kept by content hash, committed beside the work. Hmm, \"outside the repo\" — wait, let me re-read: \"Record the base run as a deviation outside the repo.\" I think this means: record it as a deviation (a record entry) for something done outside the repo (the throwaway worktree). Actually, \"a throwaway worktree\" — the test was run in a throwaway worktree with the same environment, so it's outside the repo. The record of the deviation is presumably in the ledger/record. Hmm, this is ambiguous but not clearly contradictory. The record itself (the deviation note) would live in the record. Not clearly a contradiction.\n\nLine 140: \"The job record lives in the product repo beside the code, under dot-plumbline/jobs/<job_id>/ (doctrine rule 12). Not in scratch.\" — consistent with doctrine P12.\n\nLine 143: \"The ledger is append-only... The ledger tool could not yet write generation, evaluation or amendment lines, so they were hand-appended, and that is disclosed.\" — Doctrine P12: \"Figures, hashes and record fields are printed by the tool that produced them, never retyped by hand.\" Hmm! The guide says ledger lines were hand-appended and disclosed. Doctrine says figures, hashes and record fields are printed by the tool, never retyped by hand. Is hand-appending ledger lines a contradiction? The guide discloses it as a limitation. The doctrine says \"never retyped by hand\". Hmm.\n\nBut wait — the check is about whether a reader following the document would act against the rule. Line 143 describes a past practice (\"could not yet write... so they were hand-appended, and that is disclosed\"). Is it stating a rule to hand-append? It says \"The ledger is append-only. Every amendment, generation open and close, and evaluation verdict gets a line