ished or failing parts are resumed or redone, as new commits on top. No reset.\"\n\nStory: \"the verifier part's first call was interrupted: no exit code, no reply and no harness-log entry. It was ruled LOST, which is not a failure class and does not count toward the tripwire... the Maker resumed at the verifier part only — attempt 2, with byte-identical messages, after the Owner's own yes in the Maker's chat.\"\n\nResuming with byte-identical messages after a lost call — B1 concerns length-with-empty-content, not lost calls. The tripwire counts failure classes; LOST is not a failure class. So no contradiction with A1/B1.\n\nHmm, what about doctrine C? Let me check the guide's statements against doctrine principles:\n\n1. Bar first — the guide says write the bar, freeze it. Consistent.\n\n2. Build Evaluator before Maker produces, show it catching a bad case — line 70: \"Show every referee or checker a malformed input before you trust it on a good one.\" Consistent.\n\n3. Maker never grades own work — guide consistent (Orchestrator never scores; Evaluator scores).\n\n4. Anchor to ground truth — not really addressed; not a contradiction.\n\n5. Judges tested — line 70 (malformed inputs), line 71 story. Consistent.\n\n6. Disagreement never averaged — not addressed in the guide. Not a contradiction by omission.\n\n7. Feedback carries shortfall — line 98: findings relayed unedited. Consistent.\n\n8. Feedback must be able to change next version — consistent.\n\n9. Inside a loop, a change answers only what the Evaluator reported — line 99's \"best artifact is the input\" with \"every known workable solution with the measurement behind it\". Hmm. Let me think again. The doctrine says the Evaluator does not write the revision and the Maker owns how the shortfall is fixed; a remedy only with real insight; a remedy points the way. Giving the Maker \"every known workable solution with the measurement behind it\" — that's remedies with measurement, which is more than \"a remedy points the way\" but the doctrine does allow remedies with real insight and measurement. And giving the best artifact as input — the doctrine's exits say the best version goes to the Owner on needs-owner; for loops below the cap, work goes back to the Maker. Hmm, the doctrine doesn't explicitly say the Maker gets the best artifact as input. But \"Carry the best work forward\" (P9) supports the guide's citation. The guide cites \"e-di Instrument rule 12 (I24) and the doctrine's rule to carry the best work forward\" — the doctrine does have that rule (P9: \"Carry the best work forward\"). So not a contradiction.\n\n10. Bound the loop — the guide: cap, budget, clock. Consistent.\n\n11. Escalate without interrogation — line 60, 61: plan approval checkpoints; story about decision cap. Line 61: \"a standing ruling for a defined sequence ('proceed, uncapped, three per session') saved several asks.\" Consistent with reducing Owner attention.\n\n12. Evidence or it did not happen — the guide: records, hashes, write-once. Consistent. Except the hand-appended ledger lines issue (disclosed). Hmm.\n\n13. Proportion — not addressed.\n\n14. Level of delegation — not addressed.\n\n§3 seats: the guide's combined-seat disclosure. Consistent (doctrine allows shared seats if disclosed, except Maker judging own work).\n\n§3 families: \"at least one Evaluator differs from the Maker in model family\" — the guide's cross-family pre-read is about a pre-read by a different family before freeze, which is different from the Evaluator seat family rule. The guide doesn't contradict it; it just doesn't restate it. Omission is fine.\n\nHmm wait — is there anything about the cross-family pre-read that could contradict? A3 says optional, per job, on approval sheet. Line 67 says exactly that, with recommendation guidance. Good.\n\nNow, \"verdict and disposition separate\" — line 136: \"At the cap the state is needs owner. Bring the Owner the options (amend, reject) with your recommendation\". Consistent.\n\nExits: accepted, needs owner, rejected — line 136 mentions needs owner at cap. Fine.\n\nNow, potential planted contradiction: line 90 \"[ ] Any combined seat drafted as a `separationGaps` line.\" Fine.\n\nLine 147: \"Model-judge logs are committed redacted... The full log stays outside the repo.\" Hmm — doctrine P12: \"Keep the instrument that accepted the work, and the record, with the work: committed beside it, not in local scratch.\" The guide commits redacted logs with the full log's hash beside them, full log outside the repo (presumably because it contains control code). Hmm, \"The full log stays outside the repo\" — does that contradict \"committed beside it, not in local scratch\"? The guide's reason: replies can quote control code (held-out controls must stay secret). This is a redaction practice; the guide commits redacted logs + hash. The doctrine wants the record checkable; the guide's approach balances secrecy of controls. Is this a contradiction? The doctrine says evidence committed beside the work, not in local scratch. \"Outside the repo\" could mean on the box, not in scratch. Hmm. This might be a defensible practice addition (redaction for held-out controls). The doctrine itself uses held-out controls and planted cases; secrecy of controls is inherent. I don't think this is the planted contradiction.\n\nLine 148: \"The provider key is referenced by path only. Scan for it before every push.\" Fine.\n\nLine 149: \"Git identity: author `E-DI Maker` on Maker commits, committer the Owner's recorded identity, never a helper's.\" Line 57 story: \"a different commit identity\" contradicts standing rules — consistent with line 149. Fine.\n\nLine 155: \"Dry-run the whole close... on a scratch branch worktree under the job work root.\" Under job work root — not /tmp. Good.\n\nLine 116: \"**Never put job work in `/tmp`.**\" Good.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-check line 5: \"Version of 2026-10-28.\" And line 101 references \"Owner rule of 2026-10-28 19:00 EDT,